          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Diogenes of Sinope</strong><br>\nDiogenes of Sinope was a Greek philosopher and one of the most famous figures of the philosophical school of Cynicism. He believed that virtue was better revealed in action than in theory. Diogenes practiced extreme asceticism and is said to have lived in a large ceramic jar, not a traditional home. He used his simple lifestyle and behavior to criticize the social values and institutions of what he saw as a corrupt society.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Early Life</strong><br>\nDiogenes was born in Sinope, an Ionian colony on the Black Sea, around 412 BCE. He was the son of Hicesias, a mint worker. After being exiled from Sinope for defacing currency, Diogenes moved to Athens, which was a hub of philosophical activity. It was there that he adopted the philosophy of Antisthenes, which became the foundation for Cynicism.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Philosophy of Cynicism</strong><br>\nThe Cynic philosophy advocates for a life lived according to nature, with an emphasis on simplicity and rejecting conventional desires for wealth, power, health, or fame. Diogenes taught that the path to virtue was to live a life of self-sufficiency and independence from material possessions. He is renowned for his witty remarks and for practicing parrhesia, meaning frank speech, to confront convention.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
