            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Diogenes and Alexander the Great</strong><br>\nA famous anecdote of Diogenes involves his encounter with Alexander the Great. Alexander, impressed with Diogenes' reputation, approached him and offered to grant him any wish. Diogenes, who was relaxing in the sunlight, simply asked Alexander to \"stand out of my sun,\" reflecting his disregard for authority and his commitment to a life free from luxuries.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Diogenes' Lifestyle</strong><br>\nDiogenes' lifestyle was characterized by extreme austerity and rejection of societal norms. He discarded the concept of private property and famously carried a lantern during the day, claiming to be looking for an honest man. His actions were intended to provoke thought about the nature of ethics and the folly of human vanity.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Diogenes and Plato</strong><br>\nDiogenes had a contentious relationship with Plato, another renowned philosopher of the time. On one occasion, when Plato defined a human as a \"featherless biped,\" Diogenes plucked a chicken and brought it into Plato's Academy, declaring it a human according to Plato's definition. This act was to demonstrate the absurdity of Plato's views.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
